+        // WHICH OPERAND STAYS RESIDENT, decided per call because the answer is not the same for the
+        // two things this kernel serves, and getting it wrong costs more than the tile wins.
+        //
+        // A tile loop can sweep one operand once and re-read the other per tile. Row-tile-outer reads
+        // the weights once and re-reads the activation panel; column-tile-outer does the reverse. On
+        // a core with 16 KB of L1 and no usable L2 the cheaper choice is to keep the SMALLER panel
+        // resident, so:
+        //
+        //   distilbert-ner, m=768 n=17 k=24    weights 324 KB   activations  13.5 KB  -> row-outer
+        //   a VITS conv,    m=384 n=276 k=30   weights 202 KB   activations 275 KB    -> column-outer
+        //
+        // This was column-outer unconditionally first, tuned on the convolution shape alone, and on
+        // the board that made conformer-ctc 5% SLOWER while the microbenchmark said 1.73x: at n=17 the
+        // whole activation panel fits in cache, and re-reading 324 KB of weights eight times to avoid
+        // re-reading 13.5 KB is exactly backwards. Retro-012's rule, again -- the shapes a bench holds
+        // are not the shapes a model runs.
+        const int64_t wbytes = m * k * (int64_t) sizeof(block_q4_0);
